```cpp
Elf32_Sym const *PackLinuxElf32::elf_lookup(char const *name) const
{
    if (hashtab && dynsym && dynstr) {
        unsigned const n_bucket = get_te32(&hashtab[0]);
        unsigned const *const buckets = &hashtab[2];
        unsigned const *const chains = &buckets[n_bucket];
        // Find the end of DT_HASH and DT_DYNSYM. Perhaps elf_find_table_size()
        // depends on too many valid input values?
        void const *l_hash = nullptr, *l_sym = nullptr;
        for (unsigned j = 0; j < DT_NUM; ++j) {
            void const *const ptr = dt_offsets[j] + (char const *)file_image.getVoidPtr();
            if (!l_hash && hashtab == ptr) {
                l_hash = dt_offsets[1+ j] + (char const *)file_image.getVoidPtr();
            }
            if (!l_sym && dynsym == ptr) {
                l_sym = dt_offsets[1+ j] + (char const *)file_image.getVoidPtr();
            }
            if (l_sym && l_hash)
                break;  // found both
            if (this->file_size == (off_t)dt_offsets[j])
                break;  // end sentinel
        }
        if (n_bucket) {
            unsigned const m = elf_hash(name) % n_bucket;
            unsigned n_visit = 0;
            unsigned si;
            if (l_hash <= &buckets[m])
                throwCantPack("bad DT_HASH %u", m);
            for (si= get_te32(&buckets[m]); si; si = get_te32(&chains[si])) {
                if (l_sym <= &dynsym[si])
                    throwCantPack("bad DT_HASH chain %d\n", si);
                char const *const p= get_dynsym_name(si, (unsigned)-1);
                if (p && 0==strcmp(name, p))
                    return &dynsym[si];
                if (l_sym <= &dynsym[n_visit++])
                    throwCantPack("circular DT_HASH chain %d\n", si);
            }
        }
    }
    ...
}
```

The vulnerability: The loop `for (si= get_te32(&buckets[m]); si; si = get_te32(&chains[si]))` reads `chains[si]` where `si` is attacker-controlled (from the hash chain). The check `if (l_sym <= &dynsym[si])` only checks against `l_sym` (end of dynsym), but `chains[si]` indexes into the chains table, which has a different size. If `si` is large enough, `&chains[si]` can be past the end of the chains table (and past the end of the file_image buffer), causing an OOB read via `get_te32(&chains[si])`.

The check `l_hash <= &buckets[m]` ensures `buckets[m]` is within the hash table. But there's no check that `chains[si]` is within bounds before reading it. The `l_sym <= &dynsym[si]` check is for the dynsym table, not the chains table.

So the bug: `si` from `get_te32(&buckets[m])` (or subsequent `get_te32(&chains[si])`) can be a large value that makes `&chains[si]` point past the end of the chains table (and the file_image buffer), causing an OOB read in `get_te32(&chains[si])`.

